          "category": "rent_increase_limits",
          "status": "in_force",
          "title": "Statewide cap on gross rent increases in any 12-month period",
          "requirement": "An owner of covered residential real property may not, over any 12-month period, increase the gross rental rate by more than 5 percent plus the percentage change in the cost of living, or 10 percent, whichever is lower. The base is the lowest gross rental rate charged at any time in the 12 months before the effective date of the increase, with accepted owner-offered discounts, incentives, concessions or credits excluded. The cap applies only to increases after the initial rate is set in a new tenancy where no prior tenant remains in lawful possession.",
          "citation": "Cal. Civ. Code § 1947.12(a)(1)",
          "source_url": "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1947.12",
          "quoted_span": "an owner of residential real property shall not, over the course of any 12-month period, increase the gross rental rate for a dwelling or a unit more than 5 percent plus the percentage change in the cost of living, or 10 percent, whichever is lower",
          "key_value": "Lesser of (5% + percentage change in CPI-U) or 10%",
          "coverage_conditions": {
